{
 "id": "2026-gong-perceived",
 "name": "Perceived Walking Comfort: Panorama Pairs (Macau)",
 "description": "Method replication: same task, scales and design as the paper; the images are not the paper's stimuli (49 Google Street View panoramas of Macau, all captured in December 2008, the only Google coverage of Macau; the paper used recent Baidu panoramas).\n\nGong et al. (2026, Buildings) mapped perceived walking comfort across Macau's high-density street network. Raters viewed two 2048 × 664 panoramic street views side by side and decided which appeared more comfortable for walking, with a \"same level\" option (coded 1 = left, 2 = right, 0 = equal). A guideline of eight comfort dimensions (shade and solar relief, spatial generosity, greenery and visual amenity, spatial openness and visual field, noise and traffic disturbance, cleanliness and maintenance, facility convenience, walking continuity and smoothness; Table 1) was to be weighed together as overall comfort, not one feature at a time. About 2,000 images each entered at least eight comparisons; an EfficientNet-B7 model extended the comparisons to about 6,000 images, and win/loss/tie network scores fed XGBoost/LightGBM and SHAP.\n\nThis template keeps the side-by-side panorama pair, the three answers (Image A / Same level / Image B) and the eight-dimension guideline (shown before the task and on every pair). Results ranks the panoramas with TrueSkill; ties are kept.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) images: 49 Google Street View panoramas (Macau peninsula plus four in Taipa), 2008 imagery, so streets look as they did then; the paper sampled about 6,000 Baidu panoramas at 50 m intervals; replace them with current images for a new study; (2) participants instead of four trained raters, so there is no training or pilot agreement test, only the written guideline; (3) 20 random pairs per participant instead of fixed non-overlapping batches; (4) one optional question on familiarity with Macau added.\n\nStreet images: Google Street View (© Google).",
